  data:  # 5957
    kind: invariant  # 5957
    title: "self-claims-gate is path-triggered only, so a claim regression introduced by claim-surface work hides behind green CI (63 commits measured)"  # 5957
    status: violated  # 5957
    priority: 1  # 5957
    parent:  # 5957
    tags:  # 5957
    - ci_infrastructure  # 5957
    - self_analysis  # 5957
    isbefore: []  # 5957
    duplicate_of: []  # 5957
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5957
    pr_ref:  # 5957
    description: "A CI gate whose trigger is a path include-list cannot see a regression that its\nown subject matter introduces elsewhere. Demonstrated, not hypothesised.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. The self-claims-gate step in .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml runs only\nwhen a commit touches one of: the claims files, docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/**,\ndocs/io-primitives-overlays/**, **/io_primitives/**, **/taint_sources|sinks|\nsanitizers/**, **/pyproject.toml, or the two gate scripts. Work in the 8.0.0\ncycle rowed pathlib.Path's surface in the I/O catalogue, which made Path.rename a\ncatalogued host_fs sink. Two long-standing bare Path.rename calls in\n_archive_entry immediately became unsanitized flows and runtime-cli-no-host-fs\nwent confirmed_with_caveats -> violated.\n\nIt then sat on green dev for 63 COMMITS (gate last ran at e6cb77fa15) because no\ncommit in that window touched a trigger path. It surfaced only when a version\nbump edited all seven pyproject.toml files during the v8.0.0 release, i.e. by\ncoincidence, during an unrelated operation, on a release branch.\n\nWHY THE OTHER LAYER DID NOT COVER IT. tests/../test_runtime_fs_write_wrapper_\ndiscipline.py derives its primitive set from the live catalogue and then\nsubtracts catalog.ambiguous_names. rename IS catalogued fs_write (pathlib.Path\n.rename and os.rename) and IS ambiguous, so it is filtered out there. That is\ndeliberate and documented: the guard calls itself \"a syntactic backstop for the\nunambiguous majority\" and explicitly delegates the remainder to \"the boundary\nanalysis, which has receiver evidence\". The boundary analysis IS this gate. So\nlayer 1 delegated its blind spot to a layer that was not running.\n\nCURRENT EXPOSURE, measured on dev 4799c634e9 rather than assumed: replace and\nremove remain in the same position -- catalogued fs_write, ambiguous, therefore\ninvisible to layer 1 and dependent on a gate that may be dormant. An AST scan of\nthe three runtime modules finds NO unwrapped fs-write among them today (every\n.replace call is str.replace -- two args; Path.replace takes one), so the\nexposure is latent rather than live. The next ambiguous fs-write primitive added\nto the catalogue lands in the same gap.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLOSE IT. A scheduled arm alongside the path trigger is the obvious\ncandidate -- full-suite already runs twice daily and could carry the gate, which\nwould have bounded detection at ~12 hours instead of 63 commits. Alternatives\nworth pricing before choosing: widening the trigger to any packages/**/*.py\n(cheap to state, expensive per-PR), or making the ambiguous-name set fail closed\nin layer 1 with a receiver-typed check rather than a name filter.\n\nRANKING NOTE, stated rather than smuggled: the standing owner ruling is to rank\nthe queue by the pre-registered <50% taint-precision band (WI-sivuz). This item\nis not in that band's scope -- it is CI gate coverage, not precision or recall\nwork -- so its P1 does not displace that ordering and should not be read as\ndoing so. Filed P1 because it is the mechanism by which a security-claim\nregression survived 63 commits behind a green pipeline."  # 5957
    fields:  # 5957
      statement: "A self-claim regression must become visible to CI within a bounded number of commits of its introduction, regardless of which paths the introducing commit happens to touch."  # 5957
      root_cause: "The self-claims-gate step in .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml is conditioned on a path include-list. A commit that changes claim-relevant behaviour without touching one of those paths does not run the gate, and the AST discipline guard that would otherwise catch fs-write regressions explicitly delegates its ambiguous-name blind spot (rename, replace, remove) to this same gate."  # 5957
- op: discuss  # 4164
  at: '2026-08-21T18:45:58Z'  # 4164
  by: agent  # 4164
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4164
  clock: 2  # 4164
  nonce: '4164'  # 4164
  message: "ROOT CAUSE REFUTED BY CI LOGS, 2026-08-21, dev 6e290fcdb8. Every claim below is\nread out of a real CI run via scripts/ci-debug, not inferred from the workflow files.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S ROOT_CAUSE IS FALSE AS WRITTEN, and its proposed remedy was ALREADY\nSHIPPED FOUR DAYS BEFORE THE ITEM WAS FILED.\n\n1. THE GATE HAS TWO ARMS, NOT ONE. The root_cause says the gate \"is conditioned on\n   a path include-list\". That is true of the per-PR arm (.woodpecker/woodpecker.yml\n   :353). There is a SECOND arm in .woodpecker/full-suite.yml:138 with NO when-block\n   at all, added by 097ab4e05a on 2026-08-16 03:31 -- which is 42 MINUTES BEFORE\n   e6cb77fa15 (08-16 04:13), the commit this item names as the start of its\n   63-commit window. The unconditional scheduled arm was live for the ENTIRE window.\n\n2. THE REMEDY THIS ITEM PROPOSES IS THAT ARM. \"A scheduled arm alongside the path\n   trigger is the obvious candidate -- full-suite already runs twice daily and could\n   carry the gate, which would have bounded detection at ~12 hours instead of 63\n   commits.\" It did carry the gate. It did not bound detection.\n\n3. THE CRON ARM RAN AND WAS RIGHT. Fetched log, cron firing of 2026-08-19 01:00 UTC,\n   tip d555891ab2, job self-claims-gate:\n\n     + PYTHON=python scripts/check-self-claims\n     check-self-claims: analysing hypergumbo with hypergumbo ...\n     check-self-claims-drift: SELF-CLAIM DRIFT\n       REGRESSED: runtime-cli-no-host-fs [verdict]: \"confirmed_with_caveats\" -> \"violated\"\n       changed: runtime-cli-no-host-fs [caveats]: [\"opaque_boundary\", \"user_supplied_sanitizer\"] -> []\n\n   That is this item's regression, named exactly, ~34 hours before the release that\n   the item credits with surfacing it. Detection was NOT the failure.\n\nSO WHAT ACTUALLY BREACHED THE INVARIANT IS SIGNAL DELIVERY, and it is three\nindependent mechanisms, all measured:\n\n   M1 MASKING. The cron publishes ONE aggregate commit status,\n      ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite. At the 2026-08-20 13:00 firing (tip f7d88b98e7)\n      that status is FAILURE while self-claims-gate PASSED in the same run\n      (\"check-self-claims-drift: OK -- 18 claims unchanged\"). The failing sibling is\n      test-all-packages, on ONE tracker TUI test:\n        FAILED packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/test_tui.py::TestLockScreenUnit::\n        test_cancel_returns_none - AssertionError: assert 'NOT_SET' is None\n        = 1 failed, 23210 passed ... in 935.34s\n      A red aggregate therefore carries no information about the gate. Anyone who\n      looked on 08-19 would have attributed the red to the known-red sibling.\n\n   M2 ADDRESSING. A cron status lands on whatever commit was dev's tip at cron time,\n      which is never HEAD by the time anyone looks. The project's own CI reader,\n      scripts/ci-debug (_cmd_runs_github), does sha=$(git rev-parse HEAD) and renders\n      only HEAD's statuses. At this writing HEAD 6e290fcdb8 shows ONLY\n      ci/woodpecker/push/woodpecker: success -- so the instrument reports \"green\"\n      while carrying no information at all about the last cron verdict. THIS is why\n      dev read green for 63 commits.\n\n   M3 ERROR IS NOT FAILURE. This item states the gate \"last ran at e6cb77fa15\". It\n      did not COMPLETE there. The fetched log for that run ends at\n      \"check-self-claims: analysing hypergumbo with hypergumbo ...\" with no verdict\n      line, matching its commit status of ERROR (not failure). A pipeline that dies\n      mid-gate is indistinguishable, downstream, from one whose gate passed.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG. The item's alternative \"widening the trigger to any\npackages/**/*.py\" contradicts a deliberate, documented decision recorded at\nwoodpecker.yml:337-341: catalogue/claims/dependency changes \"pay the ~10-minute\nself-analysis per-PR; analyzer .py changes stay on the cron full-suite cadence (a\ndeliberate scoping decision -- gating every core PR on a self-analysis was judged\ntoo heavy)\". The design already anticipated this item's complaint and chose the cron\nas the fallback. The cron fallback works. Its VERDICT DELIVERY does not.\n\nNOT YET ESTABLISHED, said plainly: I have not identified which commit introduced the\nregression (the pathlib surface rowing is most likely 12133e4ad0, 08-15, which\nPREDATES both arms existing -- the gate \"had been running nowhere\" until 08-16, so\nthe introducing commit could not have been caught by any arm). If that is right, the\nper-PR path filter never had a chance to fire on the introducing commit and M1-M3\nare the whole story."  # 4164
